#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392224 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "787173e1-f957-5ff0-9d14-a6ccb11de1d8" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Matrix Polynomial Using the Cayley-Hamilton Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "de6c8aef-a70b-571d-ad06-c343dc69c199" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-10 & 40 \\\\ 0 & -10\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392226} {:answer/id #uuid "cb7c199c-9d40-5982-8cd7-d65d993b6a6e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-20 & 22 \\\\ -42 & 20\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392227} {:answer/id #uuid "4ad98495-909a-50b7-aa42-65cbbef7dca9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}18 & 0 \\\\ 40 & -22\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392228} {:answer/id #uuid "862263ee-5ba6-55a2-96fe-c5e3f12585cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-20 & 40 \\\\ 0 & 20\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392229} {:answer/id #uuid "bec74d34-14b4-582b-b3b2-0b5244b0f153" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-18 & 0 \\\\ -40 & 22\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392230}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bec74d34-14b4-582b-b3b2-0b5244b0f153" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-18 & 0 \\\\ -40 & 22\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392230} :answer-field/id #uuid "b597e5da-93f2-5924-a068-9852635bdc5a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392225}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6884fbdf-c141-559a-9c69-73ca1eb1083a" :question/math-academy-id "q-38727" :question/problem "Given the matrix\n$\\,A=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 10 & -5\\end{aligned}]$\nand $f(x)={x}^{23}-25{x}^{21}-4x+2$, find $f(A).$" :question/worked-solution "Let's start by finding the characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda ):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(\\lambda ) & =\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I) \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}5-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 10 & -5-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(5-\\lambda )(-5-\\lambda ) \\\\  & ={\\lambda }^{2}-25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's divide $f(x)$ by $p(x)$ using long division:\n\n|  | $\\,\\,{x}^{21}$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${x}^{2}-25\\,$ | $\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{{x}^{23}\\,-\\,25{x}^{21}\\,-\\,4x\\,+\\,2}$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n|  | ${x}^{23}$ | $-$ | $25{x}^{21}$ |  |  |  |  |\n|  |  |  |  | $-$ | $4x$ | $+$ | $2$ |\n\nTherefore, we can write $f(x)$ as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =q(x)p(x)+r(x) \\\\  & =q(x)p(x)+(-4x+2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $q(x)={x}^{21}.$ Replacing $x$ with $A,$ we have\n\n$$\nf(A)=q(A)p(A)+(-4A+2I).\n$$\n\nAccording to the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we have $p(A)=O.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(A) & =q(A)p(A)+(-4A+2I) \\\\  & =q(A)\\cdot O+(-4A+2I) \\\\  & =-4A+2I \\\\  & =-4[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 10 & -5\\end{aligned}]+2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-20 & 0 \\\\ -40 & 20\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-18 & 0 \\\\ -40 & 22\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392231 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "787173e1-f957-5ff0-9d14-a6ccb11de1d8" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Matrix Polynomial Using the Cayley-Hamilton Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ee48dc36-244e-5623-8162-9e5def5de475" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}18 & -22 \\\\ 6 & -6\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392233} {:answer/id #uuid "4e10d15f-edfd-50e4-802f-39336ec76684" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-12 & -22 \\\\ 6 & 12\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392234} {:answer/id #uuid "7b98b20b-4948-5630-912d-d10854621cc9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-6 & -22 \\\\ 6 & 18\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392235} {:answer/id #uuid "e5ce0307-f7b7-5df0-93a8-a3b0f9edcea8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-6 & -22 \\\\ 18 & 6\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392236} {:answer/id #uuid "52829169-8b9a-5477-a7ca-27875b4c0680" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-22 & -6 \\\\ 6 & 18\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392237}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7b98b20b-4948-5630-912d-d10854621cc9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-6 & -22 \\\\ 6 & 18\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392235} :answer-field/id #uuid "f2234961-7a5f-5c89-bdd3-f09c2cf9ab03" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392232}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4c6e1cb3-d3e5-5ac7-9b61-9f6c0c45ae57" :question/math-academy-id "q-38728" :question/problem "Given the matrix\n$\\,A=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 11 \\\\ -3 & -6\\end{aligned}]$\nand $f(x)={x}^{15}-3{x}^{13}+{x}^{3}-5x+6$, find $f(A).$" :question/worked-solution "Let's start by finding the characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda )$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(\\lambda ) & =\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I) \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}6-\\lambda  & 11 \\\\ -3 & -6-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(6-\\lambda )(-6-\\lambda )+33 \\\\  & ={\\lambda }^{2}-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's divide $f(x)$ by $p(x)$ using long division:\n\n|  | $\\,\\,{x}^{13}$ | $+$ | $x$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${x}^{2}-3\\,$ | $\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{{x}^{15}-3{x}^{13}+{x}^{3}-5x+6}$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n|  | ${x}^{15}$ | $\\,\\,\\,\\,-$ | $\\,\\,\\,\\,3{x}^{13}$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n|  |  |  |  | ${x}^{3}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,$ | $-\\,\\,$ | $\\,\\,5x$ | $\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,+$ | $\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,6$ |\n|  |  |  |  | ${x}^{3}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,$ | $-\\,\\,$ | $\\,\\,3x$ |  |  |\n|  |  |  |  |  | $-\\,\\,$ | $\\,\\,2x$ | $\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,+$ | $\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,6$ |\n\nTherefore, we can write $f(x)$ as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =q(x)p(x)+r(x) \\\\  & =q(x)p(x)+(-2x+6)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $q(x)={x}^{13}+x.$ Replacing $x$ with $A,$ we have\n\n$$\nf(A)=q(A)p(A)+(-2A+6I).\n$$\n\nAccording to the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we have $p(A)=O.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(A) & =q(A)p(A)+(-2A+6I) \\\\  & =q(A)\\cdot O+(-2A+6I) \\\\  & =-2A+6I \\\\  & =-2[\\begin{aligned}6 & 11 \\\\ -3 & -6\\end{aligned}]+6[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-12 & -22 \\\\ 6 & 12\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & -22 \\\\ 6 & 18\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129703 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "86028fae-a119-5194-975d-a6d9fad6faf5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Matrix of a Linear Transformation Relative to a Given Basis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "34b8c4b6-b53f-5fd6-8a59-c4c18583dd9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-25 & -14 \\\\ 16 & 9\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392239} {:answer/id #uuid "eaa442b6-3b37-5157-88fd-f23ac8a4d42a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 & -18 \\\\ 2 & -7\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392240} {:answer/id #uuid "700fe7c1-5ebe-5a72-b683-b35432a45b97" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392241} {:answer/id #uuid "7362374a-a152-5b2b-adfe-f73b7788011b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 & -10 \\\\ -4 & 13\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392242} {:answer/id #uuid "991e6247-97fb-5467-ad97-075e201709bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}11 & 6 \\\\ -20 & -11\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392243}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eaa442b6-3b37-5157-88fd-f23ac8a4d42a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 & -18 \\\\ 2 & -7\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392240} :answer-field/id #uuid "1ffe2e2a-b586-5e41-933b-844875782379" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392238}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ef695398-bdfe-5a30-85b6-97e0a8733294" :question/math-academy-id "q-38761" :question/problem "$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}11 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]},\\,C={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]},\\,[T]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the bases $B$ and $C$ of ${R}^{2},$ and the matrix $[T]_{B}$ of a linear transformation $T$ with respect to the basis $B$, shown above. Find the matrix of $T$ relative to the basis $C.$" :question/worked-solution "The connection between the matrices of our linear transformation $T$ relative to the bases $B$ and $C$ is given by the following diagram:\n\n![](images/1b/1b81504d6fdcabee0e1f586fef3dd2af5a57a8411ba4d22429e00e09b171a4d1.png)\n\n \n\nHere, moving along an arrow means pre-multiplying by the corresponding matrix. Algebraically, we can write this as\n\n$$\n[T]_{C}={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1},\n$$\n\nwhere ${P}_{B\\to C}$ is the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C.$\n\nFirst, let's denote the vectors of $B$ and $C$ as ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ and ${{c}_{1},{c}_{2}}$, respectively.\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of vectors from $B$ relative to the basis $C.$\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ relative to the basis $C.$ To do this, we consider the matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWe reduce $M$ using Gaussian elimination until we get the identity matrix on the left. By doing this, we obtain the required change-of-coordinates matrix on the right.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 11 & 6 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -5 & -3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 11 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 6 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 11 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 7 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to C}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} & ={[\\begin{aligned}7 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1\\cdot 7-2\\cdot 4}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 \\\\ -2 & 7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -7\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can compute the matrix of $T$ relative to the basis $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T]_{C} & ={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & -18 \\\\ 2 & -7\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392244 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "84947238-f446-5261-8ecd-ee263f64e04f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Image of a Vector Under a Linear Map Given the Matrix Relative to Two Bases"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "085effc3-c783-5676-967e-97129111b4f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-26 \\\\ 22\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392246} {:answer/id #uuid "f4a58ad5-e8ee-54fb-8b09-0dbc72ae0b73" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-22 \\\\ 26\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392247} {:answer/id #uuid "cc4b2e81-7b70-5ade-85cf-7ea065025842" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392248} {:answer/id #uuid "98989a1e-e9dc-544f-9778-43fe5c17b3aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 16\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392249} {:answer/id #uuid "bc26b8f6-e701-50a1-9401-a41d25540de6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392250}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f4a58ad5-e8ee-54fb-8b09-0dbc72ae0b73" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-22 \\\\ 26\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392247} :answer-field/id #uuid "a536e81a-236e-5f13-9590-77552e7f9f3d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392245}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "29435b44-7a2b-5c1e-b63f-b6b051a05f5b" :question/math-academy-id "q-38867" :question/problem "$$\n[T]_{B|C}=[\\begin{aligned}5 & -1 & -7 \\\\ -2 & 3 & -4\\end{aligned}],\\,[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSuppose $B$ and $C$ are bases of the vector spaces $V$ and $W,$ respectively. The matrix $[T]_{B|C}$ of the linear map $T:V\\to W$ relative to these bases, and the coordinates of the vector $x\\in V$ relative to $B$ are shown above. Find $[T(x)]_{C}.$" :question/worked-solution "The coordinates of the image of $x$ under $T$ relative to the basis $C$ can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T(x)]_{C} & =[T]_{B|C}\\cdot [x]_{B} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & -1 & -7 \\\\ -2 & 3 & -4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-22 \\\\ 26\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392251 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6641b2cf-00bc-5542-a566-5b8689f2f2a4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Matrix of a Linear Transformation Given the Images of the Basis Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "203ae31f-e7d7-5ba9-985d-c5c53968ef9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392253} {:answer/id #uuid "d9505477-b80c-5e60-9ad5-b8cceaf69da5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392254} {:answer/id #uuid "bc7cd5bf-81b1-5350-b65a-736745d05714" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392255} {:answer/id #uuid "4e43d5b8-d929-5ed6-8f43-093ad3710dec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392256} {:answer/id #uuid "c1643dd1-1ac2-5766-b8de-dea684f1a01d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392257}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bc7cd5bf-81b1-5350-b65a-736745d05714" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392255} :answer-field/id #uuid "1ac46a3d-440c-54c6-a48d-2372df405d0e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392252}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "217d7c6a-01fa-5c09-8fc9-462942a415a5" :question/math-academy-id "q-38884" :question/problem "Let $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ be a basis of ${R}^{2}$ and let $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ be a linear transformation. Find $[\\,T\\,]_{B}\\,$ if $\\,T({b}_{1})={b}_{2}\\,$ and $\\,T({b}_{2})=2{b}_{1}.$" :question/worked-solution "Since we have $\\,T({b}_{1})={b}_{2}\\,$ and $\\,T({b}_{2})=2{b}_{1}\\,$, we have\n\n$$\n[T({b}_{1})]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,[T({b}_{2})]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, the $B$-matrix for $T$ is a matrix  whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors $T({b}_{1})$ and $T({b}_{2})$ relative to the basis $B.$ Therefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n[\\,T\\,]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392258 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6641b2cf-00bc-5542-a566-5b8689f2f2a4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Matrix of a Linear Transformation Given the Images of the Basis Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9855ea4b-8a43-5a1c-8653-7c408402f23a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392260} {:answer/id #uuid "06429150-0866-5904-9e4f-1a356acc29f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392261} {:answer/id #uuid "592df18c-e085-5080-ae4f-853660f4fe35" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392262} {:answer/id #uuid "ce6d8df9-fa37-5e8b-b260-0b8ebcc189c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392263} {:answer/id #uuid "73276445-d0a0-5997-9351-20bbb0bf30e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 2 & -5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392264}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "06429150-0866-5904-9e4f-1a356acc29f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392261} :answer-field/id #uuid "1547161f-c853-5ea7-9655-8e01407bf004" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392259}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a4116c45-97e6-56c3-98ac-05554e466b38" :question/math-academy-id "q-38913" :question/problem "Let $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ be a basis of ${R}^{2}$ and let $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ be a linear transformation. Find $[\\,T\\,]_{B}\\,$ if $\\,T({b}_{1})={b}_{2}-2{b}_{2}+{b}_{1}\\,$ and $\\,T({b}_{2})=3{b}_{2}+2{b}_{1}-5{b}_{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "Since we have $\\,T({b}_{1})={b}_{1}-{b}_{2}\\,$ and $\\,T({b}_{2})=2{b}_{1}-2{b}_{2}\\,$, we have\n\n$$\n[T({b}_{1})]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,[T({b}_{2})]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, the $B$-matrix for $T$ is a matrix  whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors $T({b}_{1})$ and $T({b}_{2})$ relative to the basis $B.$ Therefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n[\\,T\\,]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392265 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "83fad21d-6b36-5198-a13f-362d34342475" :knowledge-point/title "Multiplying Complex Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "54c5bfcf-f18d-59e1-a9c7-3d228f60eaf0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4-\\text{i} & 3+12\\text{i} \\\\ 2+4\\text{i} & 4+\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392267} {:answer/id #uuid "cf089755-1d10-500b-a0a2-551f3bc3019b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-4-\\text{i} & 3+12\\text{i} \\\\ 2+4\\text{i} & 4+\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392268} {:answer/id #uuid "a3e63d90-d955-5848-a2e2-a18e3e4f0166" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4+\\text{i} & -3-12\\text{i} \\\\ -2-4\\text{i} & -4-\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392269} {:answer/id #uuid "a9746754-47aa-55a4-a6fd-ff02ba352d82" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-4-\\text{i} & 3+12\\text{i} \\\\ 4+2\\text{i} & 1+\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392270} {:answer/id #uuid "e8b99d12-86e4-57b5-bce0-9cfb8912ad79" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\text{i} & 12\\text{i} \\\\ 2+4\\text{i} & 4+\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392271}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cf089755-1d10-500b-a0a2-551f3bc3019b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-4-\\text{i} & 3+12\\text{i} \\\\ 2+4\\text{i} & 4+\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392268} :answer-field/id #uuid "7064abf3-62e0-5e1b-afc9-1e54dab5330e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392266}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "469cfb30-4bd8-5f24-a8af-f1faf991da16" :question/math-academy-id "q-39021" :question/problem "If $\\,A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5-\\text{i} \\\\ \\text{i} & -3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}1-2\\text{i} & 1+2\\text{i} \\\\ -1 & 2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]\\,$, then $AB=$" :question/worked-solution "Multiplying the matrices using the usual method, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5-\\text{i} \\\\ \\text{i} & -3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1-2\\text{i} & 1+2\\text{i} \\\\ -1 & 2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}(1)(1-2\\text{i})+(5-\\text{i})(-1) & (1)(1+2\\text{i})+(5-\\text{i})(2\\text{i}) \\\\ (\\text{i})(1-2\\text{i})+(-3\\text{i})(-1) & (\\text{i})(1+2\\text{i})+(-3\\text{i})(2\\text{i})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}(1-2\\text{i})+(-5+\\text{i}) & (1+2\\text{i})+(2+10\\text{i}) \\\\ (2+\\text{i})+(3\\text{i}) & (-2+\\text{i})+(6)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4-\\text{i} & 3+12\\text{i} \\\\ 2+4\\text{i} & 4+\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392272 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b049dea4-5045-57ec-ad6a-c49f30e5da1f" :knowledge-point/title "Converting a Matrix to Rotation-Scaling Form Given Multiple Parts of the Decomposition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9e0e53bd-8745-5da4-abb6-0168c5f3b7d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392274} {:answer/id #uuid "476bc4f8-15ca-5f43-b421-6be5db9f13ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392275} {:answer/id #uuid "1e73dd85-d5f3-51a5-becd-e240b1df303b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392276} {:answer/id #uuid "67c66a85-607a-5c01-b5d7-7cb5992959b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392277} {:answer/id #uuid "2b845846-0d25-558e-832d-645c178ab7ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392278}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "67c66a85-607a-5c01-b5d7-7cb5992959b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392277} :answer-field/id #uuid "90d04520-0124-5203-a746-6e09d2550e03" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392273}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3f1bb626-14db-51ca-a15c-04bb2468d62d" :question/math-academy-id "q-39124" :question/problem "$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{i}-1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the vector $v$ and the rotation-scaling matrix $C$ shown above. Given that $v$ is an eigenvector of a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1+2\\text{i},$ find a matrix $P$ such that $A=PC{P}^{-1}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda .$\n\nFirst, notice that \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{aligned}]=C.\n$$\n\nNext, we can find the real and imaginary parts of our eigenvector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =[\\begin{aligned}\\text{i}-1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]={{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Re}(v)}+\\text{i}{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Im}(v)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can now construct $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129718 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1dcdd3f4-c03e-5887-a493-05598dbe4fc3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors of a Matrix in Rotation-Scaling Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a44df2bc-f14f-59a2-a672-6f0e6bb9920f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$λ = 5∓2\\textrm{i}$ and $v = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 ± 4\\textrm{i} \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127760} {:answer/id #uuid "df507133-b25f-5b28-ba47-8c4a2f3d63c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$λ = 2 ± 5\\textrm{i}$ and $v = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 ± 4\\textrm{i} \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127761} {:answer/id #uuid "12c35eb9-f22b-5303-ba37-a36c95e38a27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$λ =-1∓4\\textrm{i}$ and $v = \\begin{bmatrix}2 ± 5\\textrm{i} \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127762} {:answer/id #uuid "137bdf37-6094-5b37-bef7-d0150c3ee24f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$λ = 5∓2\\textrm{i}$ and $v = \\begin{bmatrix}4 ± \\textrm{i} \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127763} {:answer/id #uuid "1ea63856-d4a2-5c1e-848c-4ccd15dd67b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$λ =-1 ± 4\\textrm{i}$ and $v = \\begin{bmatrix}5 ± 2\\textrm{i} \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127764}] :answer-field/id #uuid "691b3425-43de-571e-82e4-0da7a888fe7c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127759}] :question/id #uuid "a0d796fd-8159-54fb-b817-7bb654ebaf53" :question/math-academy-id "q-39130" :question/problem "$P = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}, C = \\begin{bmatrix}5 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}$\nConsider the matrix $P$ and the rotation-scaling matrix $C$ shown above. Given that $A = PCP^{-1}$, what are the eigenvalues and the corresponding eigenvectors of $A$?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167321 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cec0e48f-9fbb-5ada-b7fd-0d389d1b7438" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem for a Decoupled Linear System of First-Order ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "de508fec-bb0b-58d2-bf06-85a976e7ffd7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392280} {:answer/id #uuid "732a2b72-2cce-5577-8457-2d4aa0265d99" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392281} {:answer/id #uuid "c0d4bc66-ebbb-5809-893f-d40899db4490" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392282} {:answer/id #uuid "c47107e6-5313-5cca-bc92-ca6cf6974486" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392283} {:answer/id #uuid "980c9999-b2c3-5122-aeca-e673911c9695" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392284}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "de508fec-bb0b-58d2-bf06-85a976e7ffd7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392280} :answer-field/id #uuid "c79029eb-544c-5d0f-a684-fd3784382b88" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392279}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "101d2691-d73f-5028-af43-74b860b5e6e3" :question/math-academy-id "q-39239" :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]x(t),\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We have a decoupled system. Therefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial condition $x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(0) & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =-1\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+1\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167322 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cec0e48f-9fbb-5ada-b7fd-0d389d1b7438" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem for a Decoupled Linear System of First-Order ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aa3db26d-febd-58a1-b616-1d6b522d8aab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]{2}^{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392286} {:answer/id #uuid "9620beff-667e-5109-984d-2884bf410cfe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2) \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]{2}^{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392287} {:answer/id #uuid "5233dc81-dfd0-5b93-b840-6b74f7a55d25" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]{2}^{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392288} {:answer/id #uuid "e10d5df6-f9ee-51e8-9408-ae84e1eeeb49" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)\\end{aligned}]{2}^{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392289} {:answer/id #uuid "c142b274-a523-5521-9fa8-bedeebe078ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2) \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]{2}^{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392290}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5233dc81-dfd0-5b93-b840-6b74f7a55d25" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]{2}^{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392288} :answer-field/id #uuid "2823b972-1160-5b63-87f9-acb86afa0db7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392285}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f661d2d1-605b-537c-9525-c7b60f731250" :question/math-academy-id "q-39268" :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)\\end{aligned}]x(t),\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We have a decoupled system. Therefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{0t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)t} \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{2}^{t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial condition $x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(0) & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{2}^{0} \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =5[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{2}^{t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]{2}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392291 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cec0e48f-9fbb-5ada-b7fd-0d389d1b7438" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem for a Decoupled Linear System of First-Order ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3e9d9a47-3583-59c2-a4a5-d9b7cfbabc24" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{\\sqrt{6}t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392293} {:answer/id #uuid "932c6061-7cb9-580f-acce-6047383bf69d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{6} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{\\sqrt{6}t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392294} {:answer/id #uuid "690d1f44-18ea-5111-91c1-cd804b8318f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{\\sqrt{6}t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392295} {:answer/id #uuid "3046ed62-20a1-55c9-81f9-f2e7be7aafa7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{\\sqrt{6}t}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392296} {:answer/id #uuid "820e88f3-13a8-52c4-9120-9c26a28538cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{6} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{\\sqrt{6}t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392297}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3e9d9a47-3583-59c2-a4a5-d9b7cfbabc24" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{\\sqrt{6}t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392293} :answer-field/id #uuid "dbd8613b-a6de-5527-afe3-03ff9a9e544b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392292}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e6e051aa-62ce-5cb4-9eb2-83e2d1aaa8ea" :question/math-academy-id "q-39269" :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{6} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]x(t),\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We have a decoupled system. Therefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{\\sqrt{6}t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial condition $x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(0) & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =2[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{\\sqrt{6}t}+0\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{\\sqrt{6}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392298 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6d3a0d58-20a3-570d-91e7-aa8f10dafb5a" :knowledge-point/title "Writing a Triple Integral as a Repeated Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "da4f271c-05bb-560b-a74d-818972696a0e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{4}(x+2y)^{2}\\,\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392300} {:answer/id #uuid "e56914da-98bd-58e4-a9d6-f24a5e42a2d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{4}(x+2y)^{2}\\,\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392301} {:answer/id #uuid "a61c939a-d213-538d-afea-78b76c2a371e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{3}(x+2y)^{2}\\,\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392302} {:answer/id #uuid "e52dad50-de38-5242-8d16-aebb63eacbba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(x+2y)^{2}\\,\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392303} {:answer/id #uuid "a82ccdba-170f-56c5-8dab-4a09f8a33a57" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(x+2y)^{2}\\,\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392304}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a82ccdba-170f-56c5-8dab-4a09f8a33a57" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(x+2y)^{2}\\,\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392304} :answer-field/id #uuid "4cc63367-da6f-59db-bfe0-c6024316c46e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392299}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2538bdad-281c-5cc7-951e-592c78afc26b" :question/math-academy-id "q-39478" :question/problem "Express the triple integral\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}(x+2y)^{2}\\,\\,\\text{d}V\n$$\n\nover the box\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 4,\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,0\\le z\\le 3}\n$$\n\nas a repeated integral of the form \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{c}^{d}{\\int }_{e}^{f}…\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Given the limits for $x,y,$ and $z,$ we can express our triple integral as a repeated integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}(x+2y)^{2}\\,\\text{d}V & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(x+2y)^{2}\\,\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392305 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2a962d5e-5cc9-530e-9f95-85c399af7e4b" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Decoupling Substitution of a Linear System of Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "95c071f2-d183-5d0d-884e-02458238551a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392307} {:answer/id #uuid "50375b4d-28b4-52c7-b4e8-da9e4abb9597" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392308} {:answer/id #uuid "d842ead4-dae7-578d-935a-cf5bac8c117d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392309} {:answer/id #uuid "f030fffb-a38d-5285-b3bf-8c22559708e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392310} {:answer/id #uuid "789eaf2b-dd8e-5756-a0fa-3809fed7c9da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392311}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "789eaf2b-dd8e-5756-a0fa-3809fed7c9da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392311} :answer-field/id #uuid "a618d060-9cdb-5241-8f90-2a9db5fbd04e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392306}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "547cf423-346c-5ea7-9462-268138005651" :question/math-academy-id "q-39699" :question/problem "Let ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]$ and ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ be eigenvectors of a matrix $A.$ Find the decoupling matrix of the differential equation ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we construct the matrix $E$, whose columns are the eigenvectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}:$\n\n$$\nE=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, the decoupling matrix can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={E}^{-1} \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1\\cdot (-1)-1\\cdot (-2)}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392312 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2a962d5e-5cc9-530e-9f95-85c399af7e4b" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Decoupling Substitution of a Linear System of Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "73910ac7-0fcb-5739-a4ef-ee62e3d20871" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ -5 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392314} {:answer/id #uuid "def8d191-233e-58e5-a878-e67c318f4ee7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392315} {:answer/id #uuid "feae8b00-c727-56f2-ac19-3a222094595f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392316} {:answer/id #uuid "b2139fb4-d21d-588d-a1dd-7e43744c5df6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392317} {:answer/id #uuid "24669978-edf2-51af-8c8b-d2c608cd9695" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 5 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392318}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "73910ac7-0fcb-5739-a4ef-ee62e3d20871" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ -5 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392314} :answer-field/id #uuid "b15a31f8-c915-5260-a280-5f4fee752c54" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392313}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8820e364-7215-527a-af4b-9358606a911f" :question/math-academy-id "q-39705" :question/problem "Let ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]$ and ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$ be eigenvectors of a matrix $A.$ Find the decoupling matrix of the differential equation ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we construct the matrix $E$, whose columns are the eigenvectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}:$\n\n$$\nE=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, the decoupling matrix can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={E}^{-1} \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 3\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\cdot 3-5\\cdot 1}[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ -5 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ -5 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129760 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b4a31c6-01be-5b15-88f5-9150f350e944" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Critical Points of a Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9865ec07-4ed8-5e1e-a395-9077aca11780" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the sphere $u^{2} + v^{2} + w^{2} = 1$." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128012} {:answer/id #uuid "c69a6d94-c00a-5d87-a560-a5f2f09e4ab6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the $vw -$plane." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128013} {:answer/id #uuid "2cfd4913-4ab5-5532-846b-462d3a33aa3a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the $uv -$plane." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128014} {:answer/id #uuid "3e0934b8-9acf-5844-8c0e-424cfdf4b1d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the parabolic cylinder $w^{2} = 2u$." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128015} {:answer/id #uuid "73756ee3-5c09-5f7b-b85b-8b7272cf91bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the plane $w = u$." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128016}] :answer-field/id #uuid "09a65432-6b6f-5c96-8820-a3d6b7b8d177" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128011}] :question/id #uuid "ab44c8de-700d-51cc-ab4a-3755f7926aef" :question/math-academy-id "q-39709" :question/problem "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $T$, where $T$ is defined as\n\n$T: \\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}u^{2} - v \\\\ u^{2} + v \\\\ e^{w}\\end{bmatrix}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392319 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f260476d-35d0-504a-9e0a-86f7fb0049bd" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Orthogonal Projection of a Vector onto a One-Dimensional Subspace"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f95b4d4f-3981-5f42-b982-2efedd53cda5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{21}{10} \\\\ \\frac{7}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{21}{10} \\\\ \\frac{14}{5}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392321} {:answer/id #uuid "d4e872a3-bd96-5c70-9363-8e8326468b3d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 2 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392322} {:answer/id #uuid "f9c16bd0-b9f9-50ce-ac97-c13e6b8140a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392323} {:answer/id #uuid "ba8c40a3-5c58-5bc1-b4fb-730687bbd0c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{4} \\\\ \\frac{7}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{5} \\\\ \\frac{7}{10}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392324} {:answer/id #uuid "ef3a9ceb-3c89-5c9d-bb6a-9a6b01edf884" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{14}{5} \\\\ \\frac{7}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{21}{10} \\\\ \\frac{7}{10}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392325}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ef3a9ceb-3c89-5c9d-bb6a-9a6b01edf884" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{14}{5} \\\\ \\frac{7}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{21}{10} \\\\ \\frac{7}{10}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392325} :answer-field/id #uuid "5abf987c-eb63-5491-994b-04febf46518c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392320}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9643dc7c-31e8-5469-acd7-713795ad19d4" :question/math-academy-id "q-39727" :question/problem "Let $a=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Find the orthogonal projection of $a$ onto $\\text{Span}{b}.$" :question/worked-solution "The orthogonal projection of $a$ onto $\\text{Span}{b}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{b}\\,a & =\\frac{b\\cdot a}{b\\cdot b}b \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\cdot 1+2\\cdot 2+(-3)\\cdot (-3)+1\\cdot 4}{{4}^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-3)^{2}+{1}^{2}}b \\\\  & =\\frac{4+4+9+4}{16+4+9+1}b \\\\  & =\\frac{21}{30}b \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{10}b \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{10}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{14}{5} \\\\ \\frac{7}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{21}{10} \\\\ \\frac{7}{10}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392326 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "837e0fa4-b8d3-59c1-87c7-44317adafaf9" :knowledge-point/title "Factoring a Constant Out of the Norm"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d0d2d4d6-dc3f-5ec4-b345-070545e90673" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "972" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392328} {:answer/id #uuid "e822b660-9180-5841-bd1e-6ccba58e51de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "144\\sqrt{213}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392329} {:answer/id #uuid "d56e5b45-369d-532b-8134-f2f5c77a87af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,296" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392330} {:answer/id #uuid "6c78ba36-49e9-5965-9f45-ba146c08f339" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "432\\sqrt{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392331} {:answer/id #uuid "317e9650-ed93-59c2-b464-dc94aefaff69" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "108" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392332}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d56e5b45-369d-532b-8134-f2f5c77a87af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,296" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392330} :answer-field/id #uuid "09244988-e0c2-5a11-a8f7-5319b67ecca5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392327}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7c79b45c-ccf1-5435-9a0b-cd68b68b15b1" :question/math-academy-id "q-39770" :question/problem "Given the vector $x=[\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ 2 \\\\ \\sqrt{12} \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$, find $‖144x‖.$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that we can factor a constant out of the norm, provided that we take the absolute value of the constant:\n\n$$\n‖kx‖=|k|\\cdot ‖x‖\n$$\n\nUsing this fact and the formula for the norm, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖144x‖ & =|144|‖x‖ \\\\  & =144\\sqrt{(-8)^{2}+{2}^{2}+(\\sqrt{12})^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =144\\sqrt{64+4+12+1} \\\\  & =144\\sqrt{81} \\\\  & =144\\cdot 9 \\\\  & =1\\,296.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184781 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "26b41192-9680-55c3-930c-52ff235e1800" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Orthogonal Projection of a Vector Onto the Solution Space of a Linear System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0bd04cf3-0f82-5880-915b-421baec1c8d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 14\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392334} {:answer/id #uuid "94ec87bc-b4dc-5e19-982e-5e16668b0ee9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392335} {:answer/id #uuid "037e8461-8d16-565d-8e50-1138bb404110" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392336} {:answer/id #uuid "92804d85-7803-58d2-a103-6f4edc35f482" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392337} {:answer/id #uuid "205d1fda-454a-5e6f-a815-1ed3e0770ad7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392338}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "92804d85-7803-58d2-a103-6f4edc35f482" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392337} :answer-field/id #uuid "cc4a8c9e-ca92-5f00-a377-01527563ace3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392333}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "27e616d4-1e96-59b7-b91d-2a687a44a3ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-39858" :question/problem "Given the vector $x$ and the system of linear equations below, find the projection of $x$ onto the solution space of the system.\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 14\\end{aligned}],\\,{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}-9{x}_{2}+6{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find a basis for the solution space of the system. \n\nConsider the augmented matrix $M$ of the system, which we reduce to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -9 & 6 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is $1$ pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2},\\,{x}_{3}$ are free variables. Now, from the first equation, we get ${x}_{1}=3{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3}.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]={x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $B={[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}$ is the basis of the solution space of the system.\n\nNow, we find the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the solution space of the system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {([\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 14\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {[\\begin{aligned}10 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 5\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 14\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{14}[\\begin{aligned}5 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 10\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 14\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134254 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2b2f7e06-54f8-5a9b-a926-362fb087b59c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Orthogonal Complement of a Span"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d049385d-1fd9-58ed-9c71-8d8ea861da69" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B = {\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{5} \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}, \\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{5} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131534} {:answer/id #uuid "315fb359-59d8-578e-9ef3-d82c408336ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B = {\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2}{5} \\\\ \\frac{3}{5} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131535} {:answer/id #uuid "554f8b1f-9d14-5417-85d0-07beda09cd4e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B = {\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}, \\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131536} {:answer/id #uuid "5a9fe752-5060-5706-a8ce-ed0d0a7dfe7a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B = {\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131537} {:answer/id #uuid "60fc42cc-15a1-5450-a1f9-535066d7ae8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B = {\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{5}{3} \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}, \\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131538}] :answer-field/id #uuid "439bf2f0-c1f5-5133-95fb-e74a235565d8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131533}] :question/id #uuid "b57e3584-dab2-5078-9ba7-3beaafafb71c" :question/math-academy-id "q-39953" :question/problem "Find a basis of $H^{⊥}$ if $H = Span{\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392339 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9f94b20f-9190-5851-ba54-e7687a223a41" :knowledge-point/title "Writing a Geometric Series Using Sigma Notation With Simplifications"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6eda70eb-0015-5ff6-a27a-1b8727c3b139" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{25}\\frac{20}{9}{(\\frac{5}{4})}^{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392341} {:answer/id #uuid "a203ff6f-b2fe-5f20-b108-6457b4109493" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{25}\\frac{5}{3}{(\\frac{5}{4})}^{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392342} {:answer/id #uuid "1cce853b-1567-59db-9326-5cc8ed5cb977" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{25}\\frac{5}{4}{(\\frac{4}{3})}^{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392343} {:answer/id #uuid "4aa0372a-ebce-5aaa-9c7f-c94c24418209" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{25}\\frac{5}{3}{(\\frac{4}{3})}^{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392344} {:answer/id #uuid "e060ec6a-f7ae-526e-a29c-45cf228a93cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{25}\\frac{20}{9}{(\\frac{4}{3})}^{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392345}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1cce853b-1567-59db-9326-5cc8ed5cb977" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{25}\\frac{5}{4}{(\\frac{4}{3})}^{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392343} :answer-field/id #uuid "d03ae04c-9f4c-5a5c-881b-8f27b221c975" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392340}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "23fc1c87-6114-5e7d-b310-49216689236b" :question/math-academy-id "q-40004" :question/problem "The first term of a geometric sequence is $\\frac{5}{3}$ and the common ratio is $\\frac{4}{3}.$ Written in sigma notation, what is the sum of the first $25$ terms?" :question/worked-solution "First, we must express the series in the form:\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}{a}_{1}{r}^{n-1},\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ denotes the first term of the series, $r$ denotes the common ratio, and $N$ denotes the number of terms in the series. In this case, ${a}_{1}=\\frac{5}{3},$ $r=\\frac{4}{3}$ and $N=25.$\n\nExpressing the geometric series in sigma notation, we obtain:\n\n$$\n{S}_{25}={\\sum }_{n=1}^{25}\\frac{5}{3}\\cdot {(\\frac{4}{3})}^{n-1}\\,\\,\n$$\n\nFinally, we can simplify the above using the laws of exponents, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{25}\\frac{5}{3}\\cdot {(\\frac{4}{3})}^{n-1} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{25}\\frac{5}{3}\\cdot {(\\frac{4}{3})}^{n}\\cdot {(\\frac{4}{3})}^{-1} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{25}\\frac{5}{3}\\cdot {(\\frac{4}{3})}^{-1}\\cdot {(\\frac{4}{3})}^{n} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{25}\\frac{5}{3}\\cdot \\frac{3}{4}\\cdot {(\\frac{4}{3})}^{n} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{25}\\frac{5}{4}{(\\frac{4}{3})}^{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392346 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "acb4bd03-9b89-5775-a700-e1f3ea9fa62e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Orthogonal Projection of a Vector Onto a Plane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b3e41f35-f9a3-51af-aed3-cadc7a55cbe9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392348} {:answer/id #uuid "c12aa19b-3eaf-56cc-9bcd-1fd70423d422" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ \\frac{5}{2} \\\\ \\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392349} {:answer/id #uuid "e98668e4-b7b4-5165-b775-abfba03a29a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ 3 \\\\ \\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392350} {:answer/id #uuid "8c313108-7af6-5d44-bf88-3edd8fa13470" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ \\frac{5}{4} \\\\ \\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392351} {:answer/id #uuid "79d86d3a-5442-5662-ab8e-bda1a724f157" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ \\frac{2}{5} \\\\ \\frac{2}{5}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392352}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c12aa19b-3eaf-56cc-9bcd-1fd70423d422" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ \\frac{5}{2} \\\\ \\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392349} :answer-field/id #uuid "622b7cda-79c9-5c80-96ea-60d2acfa05fd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392347}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b8a18aba-5bf5-5598-9a5a-e92c825d1e00" :question/math-academy-id "q-40192" :question/problem "Consider the vector $x$ and the plane $r=sv+tw,$ where $s,t\\in R,$ and\n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,w=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the plane given that $v⊥w.$" :question/worked-solution "Since our plane passes through the origin, it defines the subspace $S=\\text{Span}{v,w}$ in ${R}^{3}.$ \nAnd, since the set ${v,w}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by \n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x+{\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1}}$ and $\\text{Span}{{a}_{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x & =\\frac{x\\cdot v}{v\\cdot v}v=\\frac{3\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot 1+4\\cdot 1}{(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}v=\\frac{2}{3}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x & =\\frac{x\\cdot w}{w\\cdot w}w=\\frac{3\\cdot 2+1\\cdot 1+4\\cdot 1}{{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}w=\\frac{11}{6}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x+{\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{11}{6}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(4[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+11[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ \\frac{5}{2} \\\\ \\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392353 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "39b12711-22c8-5cb7-bd61-e128734e7e6f" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Components of a Vector With Respect to a Given Orthonormal Basis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8922ae3c-603f-57bf-9d13-2577f00cdf54" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{26}} \\\\ -\\frac{24}{\\sqrt{26}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392355} {:answer/id #uuid "c8d89c68-6627-5cda-aa57-5ef9affcb139" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{10}{\\sqrt{26}} \\\\ -\\frac{24}{\\sqrt{26}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392356} {:answer/id #uuid "1279d653-2d23-571b-a06b-3fafaed60ab8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -\\sqrt{26}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392357} {:answer/id #uuid "4efb1bad-1459-5810-be3f-8072372d257a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{10}{\\sqrt{26}} \\\\ -\\sqrt{26}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392358} {:answer/id #uuid "4765fe02-6447-5405-a090-2cee7b7976a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{10}{\\sqrt{26}} \\\\ \\sqrt{26}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392359}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c8d89c68-6627-5cda-aa57-5ef9affcb139" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{10}{\\sqrt{26}} \\\\ -\\frac{24}{\\sqrt{26}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392356} :answer-field/id #uuid "8f607589-dc1e-5e13-83ae-8a50bfa4dafa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392354}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d52318cf-590e-555c-bafa-19ac086a5c9f" :question/math-academy-id "q-40293" :question/problem "Given the **orthonormal** basis $B={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{26}} \\\\ -\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{26}}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{26}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{26}}\\end{aligned}]}$  of ${R}^{2},$ find $[x]_{B}$ if $x=[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ as ${q}_{1},\\,{q}_{2}.$ \n\nSince $B$ is an orthonormal basis, we have that $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$, where ${x}_{i}=x\\cdot {q}_{i}.$ Computing the components of $x,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{26}} \\\\ -\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{26}}\\end{aligned}]=-\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{26}}-\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{26}}=-\\frac{10}{\\sqrt{26}} \\\\ {x}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{26}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{26}}\\end{aligned}]=-\\frac{25}{\\sqrt{26}}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{26}}=-\\frac{24}{\\sqrt{26}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{10}{\\sqrt{26}} \\\\ -\\frac{24}{\\sqrt{26}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392360 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ad9f55b2-ce37-507f-8cb6-7d4e30e6868a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Orthogonal Projection of a Vector Onto a Subspace"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "74abe4d7-e589-5617-ab73-5aa70c9a7476" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392362} {:answer/id #uuid "ffa1264a-2a73-508f-bc53-ad361bb49d45" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392363} {:answer/id #uuid "2a9e59f7-854e-5b73-a998-750da087cf90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392364} {:answer/id #uuid "023b683d-9286-56c1-9880-82005e279d51" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392365} {:answer/id #uuid "6a7a2853-3ab5-5f8f-83a3-978af68e4e67" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392366}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6a7a2853-3ab5-5f8f-83a3-978af68e4e67" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392366} :answer-field/id #uuid "3f5082e4-d9ee-539a-9cb4-b5f824bb8ce6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392361}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5c071401-3e46-54b6-b9cb-b8280a1a58b2" :question/math-academy-id "q-40630" :question/problem "Verify whether the vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}$ are linearly independent, and find the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace spanned by the vectors ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}},$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we verify whether ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}$ are linearly independent. So, we start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}↔{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ -3 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-2{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, the pivot columns correspond to the linearly independent vectors ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ that form the basis of our span.\n\nNow, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace spanned by the linearly independent columns of a matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -3 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\,⇒\\,{A}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following projection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -3 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {([\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -3 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -3 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {[\\begin{aligned}14 & 9 \\\\ 9 & 6\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -3 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}6 & -9 \\\\ -9 & 14\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -3 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392367 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "26b41192-9680-55c3-930c-52ff235e1800" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Orthogonal Projection of a Vector Onto the Solution Space of a Linear System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c77eba07-dc07-589f-8884-be6f0d03ee21" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392369} {:answer/id #uuid "fbcee9a5-69aa-5708-aafe-a127637a0cc5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392370} {:answer/id #uuid "893aaa25-971c-54ca-85d4-517d467f5514" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392371} {:answer/id #uuid "becd0ca3-6897-543a-8f9d-60e3e356bb81" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392372} {:answer/id #uuid "38a78463-dbb9-5ab1-b897-3e1cd62bb83b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392373}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "893aaa25-971c-54ca-85d4-517d467f5514" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392371} :answer-field/id #uuid "7ca78264-e6c8-572f-88da-a3f19c5a8e15" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392368}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "52165617-f2b7-52fc-a5ce-09980394cfc1" :question/math-academy-id "q-40631" :question/problem "Given the vector $x$ and the system of linear equations below, find the projection of $x$ onto the solution space of the system.\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 13 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+8{x}_{2}-6{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the basis of the solution space of the system.\n\nConsider the augmented matrix $M$ of the system, which we reduce to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 & 3 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 8 & -6 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is $1$ pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2},{x}_{3}$ are free variables. Now, from the first equation, we get ${x}_{1}=4{x}_{2}-3{x}_{3}.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}4{x}_{2}-3{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]={x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $B={[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}$ is the basis of the solution space of the system.\n\nNow, we find the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the solution space of the system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {([\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 13 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {[\\begin{aligned}17 & -12 \\\\ -12 & 10\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}13 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{26}[\\begin{aligned}10 & 12 \\\\ 12 & 17\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}13 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392374 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9316ee08-13ad-57cf-87cf-2c7a86645aca" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Matrix that Defines an Orthogonal Projection Onto the Subspace Spanned by a Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d27fce6f-9cef-58ac-ad71-d4b8c6cad34a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{30}[\\begin{aligned}10 & -25 & 5 \\\\ -25 & 4 & -32 \\\\ 5 & -32 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392376} {:answer/id #uuid "ea3a250a-ece4-571a-8c85-70d3e25b7d9b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}25 & -10 & 5 \\\\ -10 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 5 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392377} {:answer/id #uuid "94326bee-2594-5e0a-a95f-1c590d8bec52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{30}[\\begin{aligned}5 & -10 & 25 \\\\ -10 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 25 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392378} {:answer/id #uuid "091902af-77f1-5b24-93d6-3a2b0ada82f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{30}[\\begin{aligned}25 & -10 & 5 \\\\ -10 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 5 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392379} {:answer/id #uuid "a10eb80b-1385-5e2f-8d98-5366544749ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 5 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392380}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "091902af-77f1-5b24-93d6-3a2b0ada82f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{30}[\\begin{aligned}25 & -10 & 5 \\\\ -10 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 5 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392379} :answer-field/id #uuid "301c2b26-e6a5-5c7b-89e7-caad15eed195" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392375}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "68580897-2f57-5170-bc8a-842f9489cca5" :question/math-academy-id "q-40664" :question/problem "Find a matrix that defines the orthogonal projection of ${R}^{3}$ onto the subspace spanned by $a=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the matrix ${P}_{S}=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}$ maps any vector $x\\in {R}^{n}$ onto its orthogonal projection in the subspace $S$ spanned by the columns of the matrix $A.$ \n\nIn our case, $A=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{S} & =A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{([\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={30}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{30}[\\begin{aligned}25 & -10 & 5 \\\\ -10 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 5 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134314 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2e891363-fea2-5de4-9bbb-67fddb077200" :knowledge-point/title "Representing a Double Integral as a Repeated Integral When the Limits are Given"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "983a8306-9539-5c69-a91a-f9ec960b3aa3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{-1}^{1}[\\displaystyle \\int_{1 + x^{2}}^{2x^{2}}f(x, y)dy]dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131894} {:answer/id #uuid "d687946a-103b-518e-9748-0242c3d62b5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{2x^{2}}^{1 + x^{2}}[\\displaystyle \\int_{-1}^{1}f(x, y)dy]dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131895} {:answer/id #uuid "ef28bb04-c21f-58e1-a24d-332069008b92" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1}[\\displaystyle \\int_{x^{2}}^{2x^{2}}f(x, y)dy]dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131896} {:answer/id #uuid "0dcc0810-b2ed-555e-b4eb-c95b5743e58f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{-1}^{1}[\\displaystyle \\int_{2x^{2}}^{1 + x^{2}}f(x, y)dy]dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131897} {:answer/id #uuid "31abd510-9db4-5582-bfb5-82f50efeadd6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{1 + x^{2}}^{2x^{2}}[\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1}f(x, y)dy]dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131898}] :answer-field/id #uuid "ef571092-eed9-5c94-a146-3fd22770d33c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131893}] :question/id #uuid "3a6b121d-f72a-5163-a7c3-fb8ae97613e6" :question/math-academy-id "q-40727" :question/problem "![](images/0e/0e05b575c22c78000a1ec235bb82cbfad3cd0cdfc6db370f19f094799b305949.png)\n\nIf $D$ is the finite region enclosed by the curves $y = 2x^{2}$ and $y = 1 + x^{2}$, as shown above, then $∬_(D)f(x, y)dA =$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392381 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c4034e61-63dc-5f72-8275-e9c81290767d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Graph of a Polar Circle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "daf34dcd-691c-5756-a4ac-48595ad3a189" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/8b/8bf1a9b27d362e8f44736e7210836537eb2d05e760cf654bb3da332dc2da9fd1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392383} {:answer/id #uuid "1239b38f-9435-5446-937d-574946b68942" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/bb/bb98fa6cae9226c4334773a013fe7ac0749174e1241442946aa727b9c96d0015.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392384} {:answer/id #uuid "986036ad-70fa-5556-92fe-72217fe9bc3a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/16/161e6fe0bc44415a26a2a75f78ed44e8fbadf3ec0e56773dde8c61a15ba0b035.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392385} {:answer/id #uuid "45f27d07-4089-57d8-aa8a-88d48e6b9472" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/da/dad45fcb266c0313a1b02d08eb93763bc00c103d4a1d4da48f2ef5452a27dd61.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392386} {:answer/id #uuid "5d50c7dc-6078-540f-a255-a7009d5d11fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/ea/ea7595da12f74310da86ccf44db453dadf67ccff889aaf562f461f7c7e713807.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392387}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "986036ad-70fa-5556-92fe-72217fe9bc3a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/16/161e6fe0bc44415a26a2a75f78ed44e8fbadf3ec0e56773dde8c61a15ba0b035.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392385} :answer-field/id #uuid "43a6c6de-17d3-5232-b82b-db818cc48537" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392382}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0f87f6e7-836d-57de-ad54-3c3ac690c6db" :question/math-academy-id "q-40790" :question/problem "Which of the following is the graph of the polar curve $r=8?$" :question/worked-solution "The polar curve defined by the equation $r=8$ is a circle of radius $8$ centered at the origin. Its graph is given below.\n\n![](images/16/161e6fe0bc44415a26a2a75f78ed44e8fbadf3ec0e56773dde8c61a15ba0b035.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134324 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9870ad37-3e54-5403-936c-09a321d4a0cf" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Orthogonal Basis Using the Gram-Schmidt Process"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "88ac0269-277d-507d-9a07-1704f6fb5518" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131954} {:answer/id #uuid "b96d5915-0410-55be-9066-4de7b2318b38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131955} {:answer/id #uuid "eb030ef0-0c78-56f1-b566-9e2a5d62dc86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131956} {:answer/id #uuid "12c37052-9d03-5d81-9800-bbae1a5f1125" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131957} {:answer/id #uuid "22c13a87-afc8-56f1-84a6-f98e7cbe5eea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131958}] :answer-field/id #uuid "a1c6390d-edc6-5506-afa2-22d7f3be6733" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131953}] :question/id #uuid "246d48c4-ce2d-5de1-bae4-f0a5be4d4035" :question/math-academy-id "q-40818" :question/problem "$a_{1} = \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}, a_{2} = \\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}, a_{3} = \\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -14\\end{bmatrix}, B = {[ \\mid; v_{1}; \\mid ], [ \\mid; v_{2}; \\mid ], \\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix}}$\n\nSuppose that $V = Span{a_{1}, a_{2}, a_{3}}$. Given that $B$ is an orthogonal basis for $V$, and that $B$ is derived from $V$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $\\frac{a}{b}$?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392388 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "407616a9-e448-5652-9882-a1edf19600f8" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Radial Line Given Its Polar Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "558d39e0-0ab6-5d33-b3a0-396e26bb8653" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/aa/aa72b03a85b16e3a9ef9dc3f65f4126cdee0339ac722fcfe8a1f21973796f623.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392390} {:answer/id #uuid "3fd87d8b-270f-573d-9412-5025cd44dba3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/10/10d1cdb8ecbfe7f7153618b5da57fecefbf8711d395cf3c6f5c879f3169977f9.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392391} {:answer/id #uuid "97873c7d-0308-59b1-889c-345b9fb3b8f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/80/802bb767b28c38598663d928d66338086adf08b80bc5887a7795704183ed2212.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392392} {:answer/id #uuid "bee250d6-c6dd-5d05-9b3d-34683cc111c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/85/85271401b413b162990a99e905ebecbfb4048953e77bcb5808c0b729c1fc7de3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392393} {:answer/id #uuid "521ffe4f-8b8b-5b4e-afbd-79c02578ee0b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/2b/2b88d001cbc59e0c46922b6e369644e50a02eff26c800e9d571147988357918e.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392394}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "97873c7d-0308-59b1-889c-345b9fb3b8f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/80/802bb767b28c38598663d928d66338086adf08b80bc5887a7795704183ed2212.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392392} :answer-field/id #uuid "93203f91-1d26-5d83-a83e-32f479d20351" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392389}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "15ae4fee-42a8-584b-a59a-68427466f591" :question/math-academy-id "q-40828" :question/problem "Which of the following is the graph of the polar curve  $\\theta =\\frac{11\\pi }{10}?$" :question/worked-solution "The polar curve defined by the equation $\\theta =\\frac{11\\pi }{10}$ is the ray that starts at the origin and makes a counterclockwise angle of $\\frac{11\\pi }{10}$ with the positive $x$-axis. \n\nTherefore, the angle it makes with the negative $x$-axis is \n\n$$\n\\frac{11\\pi }{10}-\\pi =\\frac{\\pi }{10}.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe correct answer is shown below.\n\n![](images/80/802bb767b28c38598663d928d66338086adf08b80bc5887a7795704183ed2212.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392395 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "38edbd80-2aef-5b3e-8dd9-de9f2fbeca9b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Triangular Factor of a QR Factorization Given the Orthogonal Factor"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f4d46fe7-9f20-5438-8833-4e642aec5804" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R=[\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{3} & -\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392397} {:answer/id #uuid "b70257ab-9b65-525e-91bc-ee86b6bb7261" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392398} {:answer/id #uuid "ec10b933-18cd-56bc-9910-9c2d3d3d750b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R=[\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{3} & \\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392399} {:answer/id #uuid "cd7ff5bf-9015-5fce-97ea-fc8f5bdf576d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R=[\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{3} & -\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392400} {:answer/id #uuid "2675dc3f-685d-5210-a1df-c1d57411573c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3} & \\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392401}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cd7ff5bf-9015-5fce-97ea-fc8f5bdf576d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R=[\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{3} & -\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392400} :answer-field/id #uuid "41b85c88-2a2e-56d0-81b5-85750fc56b04" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392396}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1cc11afa-a8f2-5bea-a1c2-faf40b2b0365" :question/math-academy-id "q-40836" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,Q=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $Q$ shown above. Find the matrix $R$ such that $A=QR$ is a QR factorization of $A.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ has orthonormal columns, pre-multiplying both sides of $A=QR$ by ${Q}^{T},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}^{T}\\cdot A & ={Q}^{T}\\cdot QR \\\\ {Q}^{T}\\,A & =({Q}^{T}\\,Q)R \\\\ {Q}^{T}\\,A & ={I}_{2}R \\\\ R & ={Q}^{T}\\,A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNote that ${Q}^{T}Q={I}_{2}$ since the columns of $Q$ are orthonormal.\n\nNow, we calculate the matrix $R:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & ={Q}^{T}A \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{3} & -\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134330 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "19ae0632-68ac-5da9-8adf-cf71d5ecbc7c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the QR Factorization of a Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fbcea61a-f7c5-5e90-95bf-e30e97fb1c5c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392403} {:answer/id #uuid "87a540ed-333f-527d-82f8-aa109a53d564" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392404} {:answer/id #uuid "10193a27-2949-56aa-bfb7-aac130ca8727" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392405} {:answer/id #uuid "2ce7f102-17d8-5df4-8505-ab11eafbcb85" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392406} {:answer/id #uuid "5f83a4ce-78d8-5078-bb57-e6fcb33b7384" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392407}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "10193a27-2949-56aa-bfb7-aac130ca8727" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392405} :answer-field/id #uuid "ac78dc7d-8434-5df2-894a-caad12e5602a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392402}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "77f48d85-1f2c-59c8-b53a-85db3bc9d5a4" :question/math-academy-id "q-40838" :question/problem "$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}]}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{q}_{11} & {q}_{12} \\\\ {q}_{21} & {q}_{22} \\\\ {q}_{31} & {q}_{32}\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{r}_{11} & {r}_{12} \\\\ 0 & {r}_{22}\\end{aligned}]}}.\n$$\n\nConsider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown above. Given that the columns ${q}_{1},{q}_{2}$ of $Q$ were obtained from the columns of $A$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, find  the value of ${q}_{22}\\cdot {r}_{12}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let ${a}_{1},{a}_{2}$ be the columns of $A.$\n\nFirst, we find an orthonormal basis of $\\text{Col}(A)=\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ using the Gram-Schmidt process.\n\n**Step 1**. We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 2**. We find the vector ${v}_{2}$ orthogonal to $\\text{Span}{{v}_{1}}$ using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{1}}{a}_{2} \\\\  & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]-(-\\frac{12}{6})[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]+2[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we normalize ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} & =\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {q}_{2} & =\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute $R:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & ={Q}^{T}\\,A \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{6} & -2\\sqrt{6} \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following QR factorization of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}]}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{6} & -2\\sqrt{6} \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}]}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, ${q}_{22}\\cdot {r}_{12}=(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}})\\cdot (-2\\sqrt{6})=2\\sqrt{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392408 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d23c4292-6fdd-5cba-a2e7-0f4d770b2309" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Least-Squares Solution of a Linear System Given in Matrix Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a562ca1-5212-5591-86cb-6225b50132bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{15}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392410} {:answer/id #uuid "c2509cfc-7b7f-5200-a03d-b586bb4c8683" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{15}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392411} {:answer/id #uuid "55f243e4-ab36-5e84-9c97-7eae10562605" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{15}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392412} {:answer/id #uuid "d4781ce6-89fb-5ea8-96ca-5cabc4f3357f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ \\frac{1}{15}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392413} {:answer/id #uuid "991464be-2a35-5a14-90ef-c8149f9e6551" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{15} \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392414}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "55f243e4-ab36-5e84-9c97-7eae10562605" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{15}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392412} :answer-field/id #uuid "d80448bd-5afe-51a5-9a45-2dab824daa79" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392409}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a5ec0799-ee29-5297-a132-7bdd3f8273e6" :question/math-academy-id "q-40889" :question/problem "Given that the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, find the least-squares solution of $Ax=b$, where \n\n$\\,$ $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "First of all, we write the given system in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{x}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]}}=\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}}\n$$\n\nWe can find the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{x}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by ${A}^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\overset{^}{x} & ={A}^{T}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{x} & =({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\\\  & ={([\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -2 & -1 & 3 & 0\\text{ }\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\,\\,\\,\\,[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -2 & -1 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 15\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -2 & -1 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{60}[\\begin{aligned}15 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -2 & -1 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{60}[\\begin{aligned}15 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{15}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134361 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "38edbd80-2aef-5b3e-8dd9-de9f2fbeca9b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Triangular Factor of a QR Factorization Given the Orthogonal Factor"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6e0be982-1b80-5738-8cd6-74f57650e028" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{14} & \\frac{7}{\\sqrt{14}} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{5}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392416} {:answer/id #uuid "8ba57cc6-4b0a-5d92-8761-d49b07038502" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{14} & 2\\sqrt{14} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{3\\sqrt{10}}{2}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392417} {:answer/id #uuid "bce0ce7a-b8f8-5ff0-8fc3-71570e52bd82" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{6} & \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{2}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392418} {:answer/id #uuid "34f1627d-9591-5047-a498-be9818dce031" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{10} & \\frac{5}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{7}{\\sqrt{14}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392419} {:answer/id #uuid "0346b6b2-402b-56a4-86b2-20a38185f353" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{7} & \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{2} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392420}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6e0be982-1b80-5738-8cd6-74f57650e028" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{14} & \\frac{7}{\\sqrt{14}} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{5}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392416} :answer-field/id #uuid "013bd0a2-7475-538a-af66-e5aa29d3dd0b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392415}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4a949ac3-27ed-549f-9f9c-cfbce0083312" :question/math-academy-id "q-41418" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,Q=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{14}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{14}} & 0 \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{14}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $Q$ shown above. Find the matrix $R$ such that $A=QR$ will give a QR factorization of $A.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ has orthonormal columns, pre-multiplying both sides of $A=QR$ by ${Q}^{T},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}^{T}\\cdot A & ={Q}^{T}\\cdot QR \\\\ {Q}^{T}\\,A & =({Q}^{T}\\,Q)R \\\\ {Q}^{T}\\,A & ={I}_{2}R \\\\ R & ={Q}^{T}\\,A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNote that ${Q}^{T}Q={I}_{2}$ since the columns of $Q$ are orthonormal.\n\nNow, we calculate the matrix $R:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & ={Q}^{T}A \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{14}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{14}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{14}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & 0 & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{14} & \\frac{7}{\\sqrt{14}} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{5}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392421 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "866ab12b-7f51-55f8-9e7b-e39f9c3b4b44" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Orthogonal Basis For the Column Space of a Matrix Given Some Orthogonal Columns"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a8a40ae-cd89-50d8-a25c-80c8f2e72194" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392423} {:answer/id #uuid "58731cd5-c497-5022-aec7-778724ff51ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392424} {:answer/id #uuid "264463ee-c13b-575c-94d3-bd01355c5b9c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392425} {:answer/id #uuid "12434e02-991d-5e35-b828-15a6dee0fac1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392426} {:answer/id #uuid "c3355d45-a72d-58af-9389-d2104265c076" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392427}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c3355d45-a72d-58af-9389-d2104265c076" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392427} :answer-field/id #uuid "c028424c-3d91-55d9-932a-ac5901ce7206" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392422}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d9b27ae0-730c-5147-ab83-3cb6142a7ece" :question/math-academy-id "q-41629" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,B={[\\begin{aligned}| \\\\ \\frac{{a}_{1}}{‖{a}_{1}‖} \\\\ |\\end{aligned}]\\,,[\\begin{aligned}| \\\\ \\frac{{a}_{2}}{‖{a}_{2}‖} \\\\ |\\end{aligned}]\\,,[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c \\\\ d\\end{aligned}]}\n$$\n\nConsider the subspace $V=\\text{Col}(A)$ spanned by the columns ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}$ of the matrix $A,$ and an **orthonormal** basis $B$ of $\\text{Col}(A),$ shown above. Given that ${a}_{1}⊥{a}_{2},$ and that $B$ is derived from $V$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $|\\,d\\,|?$" :question/worked-solution "Since the first two vectors are already orthogonal, we proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process.\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 2**: We set ${v}_{2}={a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 3**. Next, we find ${v}_{3}$ using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{3} & ={a}_{3}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{1}}\\,{a}_{3}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{2}}\\,{a}_{3} \\\\  & ={a}_{3}-\\frac{{a}_{3}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}-\\frac{{a}_{3}\\cdot {v}_{2}}{{v}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{2}}{v}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{3}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{3} & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{16}{16}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{2}{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we normalize ${v}_{3}$ dividing it by $‖{v}_{3}‖=\\sqrt{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{3} & =\\frac{{v}_{3}}{‖{v}_{3}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $|\\,d\\,|=|-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}|=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392428 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "74efec6b-6e5f-5761-a116-5c94e3fb4326" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Least-Squares Solution of a Linear System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "67f320fe-396a-55a6-bb27-1a3f5b770ee1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392430} {:answer/id #uuid "c8635233-fd1e-577d-beff-9196e75b3ecc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392431} {:answer/id #uuid "2531cfd4-e761-5c2a-93b5-c5c0d3e904c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{14} \\\\ \\frac{1}{7}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392432} {:answer/id #uuid "5dbe0276-3eb6-514f-98ee-e9513b2b1ec4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{7} \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392433} {:answer/id #uuid "d815ad2e-7dfc-57f4-a39d-ea49c417bed8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ \\frac{4}{7}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392434}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c8635233-fd1e-577d-beff-9196e75b3ecc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392431} :answer-field/id #uuid "d92ae0bd-aff1-5837-a319-be90774d548e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392429}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e0174f23-e3c1-53ee-8e26-06192fa2ac03" :question/math-academy-id "q-41779" :question/problem "Find the least-squares solution to the system of linear equations given below. You're given that the columns of the corresponding coefficient matrix are linearly independent.\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}=-2 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}=6 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First of all, we write the given system in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 3 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{x}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]}}=\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}}.\n$$\n\nWe can find the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{x}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by ${A}^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\overset{^}{x} & ={A}^{T}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{x} & =({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\\\  & ={([\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 4 & -2 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 3 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 4 & -2 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}14 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 21\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}14 \\\\ -21\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{42}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}14 \\\\ -21\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115834 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8feeacff-b07d-5f98-a765-4d8571674a7a" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Matrix Product Using the Associative Law"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "334cfb64-857e-505a-9a43-d21f8e1caef8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 7 \\\\ 3 & -6\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392436} {:answer/id #uuid "aad0983e-16d3-5538-b3b8-9b0e2aff415b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ -15\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392437} {:answer/id #uuid "83c87507-4794-5dbb-9918-43c4f3143e69" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}15 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -6\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392438} {:answer/id #uuid "6010500b-541c-5b97-806c-4f018455b4cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ -9\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392439} {:answer/id #uuid "5816752b-1281-5b14-8078-9753e15cfdd9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 15 \\\\ 3 & -9\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392440}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5816752b-1281-5b14-8078-9753e15cfdd9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 15 \\\\ 3 & -9\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392440} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a0ff826-a7be-5da5-888c-b90181819cf0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392435}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d05ca169-7c67-546f-9f08-0fce1486794c" :question/math-academy-id "q-41785" :question/problem "Calculate $ABC$, where\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & -2 \\\\ -3 & -4 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, recall the associative law\n\n$$\nABC=(AB)C=A(BC).\n$$\n\nNotice that $A$ is a $2\\times 3$ matrix, $B$ is a $3\\times 1$ matrix, and $C$ is a $1\\times 2$ matrix. Therefore, \n\n- multiplying $A$ by $B$, we will obtain a $2\\times 1$ matrix, which will require finding $2$ entries, while\n\n- multiplying $B$ by $C$, we will obtain a $3\\times 2$ matrix, which will require finding $6$ entries.\n\nSo, we find the product $AB$ first:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & -2 \\\\ -3 & -4 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply $AB$ by $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ABC & =(AB)C \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 15 \\\\ 3 & -9\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392441 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "89362140-1a17-56fd-a9ab-4da336112bee" :knowledge-point/title "Multiplying a Matrix by a Column or Row of the Identity Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2cd465a5-2228-506e-85ae-bca8f7b15782" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -3.5 \\\\ -4\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392443} {:answer/id #uuid "89c7f214-2178-5237-aa70-418be548a79a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 3\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392444} {:answer/id #uuid "b9584a78-324d-5402-b9dd-9340417fbdbe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 3\\sqrt{2} & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392445} {:answer/id #uuid "9dfc011c-f3fc-50b6-918e-d5e364adfbb2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3} \\\\ -5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392446} {:answer/id #uuid "f8d7fd08-e9dc-5175-be41-5413c7de1648" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3} & -5 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392447}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2cd465a5-2228-506e-85ae-bca8f7b15782" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -3.5 \\\\ -4\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392443} :answer-field/id #uuid "04d1601d-b94a-56ad-af90-0c952d160d14" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392442}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7d52bfbe-0cf7-5e74-a083-e7db8c4ef851" :question/math-academy-id "q-41943" :question/problem "Find the product $AB$, if\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3} & -5 & 5 \\\\ -5 & 3\\sqrt{2} & -3.5 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -4\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}]$\nand \n$B=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $B$ is the $3$rd *column* of the identity matrix ${I}_{3}.$\n\nSince we're multiplying the $3\\times 3$ matrix $A$ by the $3$rd *column* of the identity matrix ${I}_{3}$, the result must be the $3$rd column of $A.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nAB=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -3.5 \\\\ -4\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nLet's check the long way:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3} & -5 & 5 \\\\ -5 & 3\\sqrt{2} & -3.5 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -4\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3}\\cdot 0+(-5)\\cdot 0+5\\cdot 1 \\\\ (-5)\\cdot 0+3\\sqrt{2}\\cdot 0+(-3.5)\\cdot 1 \\\\ 1\\cdot 0+2\\cdot 0+(-4\\sqrt{5})\\cdot 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -3.5 \\\\ -4\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101413 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "89362140-1a17-56fd-a9ab-4da336112bee" :knowledge-point/title "Multiplying a Matrix by a Column or Row of the Identity Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e77b0e35-b8ea-5618-ab6c-8503315df0e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3.5 \\\\ -2.5 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392449} {:answer/id #uuid "b90bbe8b-d8e5-5080-9aa2-4142743a4b96" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{7} \\\\ -2 \\\\ \\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392450} {:answer/id #uuid "6b52aa96-4f70-58ea-b2cf-28f779b7b43b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2\\sqrt{5} & -2.5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392451} {:answer/id #uuid "d06d67b6-8acb-58a8-957c-d55d012736a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\sqrt{5} \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392452} {:answer/id #uuid "e47cb90f-8189-59b3-96b0-469513eb54fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{7} & -3 & 3.5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392453}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b90bbe8b-d8e5-5080-9aa2-4142743a4b96" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{7} \\\\ -2 \\\\ \\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392450} :answer-field/id #uuid "3cb1f592-094a-5b91-bac8-80971fe96ac3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392448}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bde0cdd0-7dbe-5ba4-9b36-e66cfe099501" :question/math-academy-id "q-41949" :question/problem "Find the product $AB$, if\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{7} & -3 & 3.5 \\\\ -2 & 2\\sqrt{5} & -2.5 \\\\ \\sqrt{3} & 4 & 5\\end{aligned}]$\nand \n$B=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $B$ is the $1$st *column* of the identity matrix ${I}_{3}.$\n\nSince we're multiplying the $3\\times 3$ matrix $A$ by the $1$st *column* of the identity matrix ${I}_{3},$ the result must be the $1$st *column* of $A.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nAB=[\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{7} \\\\ -2 \\\\ \\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nLet's check the long way:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =[\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{7} & -3 & 3.5 \\\\ -2 & 2\\sqrt{5} & -2.5 \\\\ \\sqrt{3} & 4 & 5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{7}\\cdot 1+(-3)\\cdot 0+3.5\\cdot 0 \\\\ (-2)\\cdot 1+2\\sqrt{5}\\cdot 0+(-2.5)\\cdot 0 \\\\ \\sqrt{3}\\cdot 1+4\\cdot 0+5\\cdot 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{7} \\\\ -2 \\\\ \\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069340 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "05669abb-838d-5b1c-b97f-833a28b1d413" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intersection Points in the Case of One or No Solutions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8ffa3959-bcae-53d4-b750-3acd046f4817" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067181} {:answer/id #uuid "d6f13b0a-3767-5aac-9371-497ed9cc05f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067182} {:answer/id #uuid "15c4250c-ec22-5c0f-9869-16bf0a21f119" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067183} {:answer/id #uuid "76e00e67-6b8c-5712-9e4a-778c86b82f69" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Undefined because the curve and the line do not intersect." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067184} {:answer/id #uuid "7c894e31-8bab-5c7b-bdc4-511f737f9090" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067185}] :answer-field/id #uuid "f5ce676b-13f0-56a5-846b-a845f49e5220" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067180}] :question/id #uuid "801b9317-386e-554f-9207-4d232469922d" :question/math-academy-id "q-41957" :question/problem "If the line $y =-2x + 3$ and the curve $y = \\frac{2}{x - 2} + 1$ intersect at the point $(p, q)$, then $p + q =$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392454 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8feeacff-b07d-5f98-a765-4d8571674a7a" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Matrix Product Using the Associative Law"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bc44afbe-5d76-5e09-9981-f46801ba7f6a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392456} {:answer/id #uuid "52743f64-5575-5f50-ab83-057fdf07b94a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392457} {:answer/id #uuid "56be43e4-cbda-5ce6-b53d-23acb1dfe969" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 & -3 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392458} {:answer/id #uuid "77068922-3e71-5bec-a56b-febc23200124" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392459} {:answer/id #uuid "55501deb-48c2-5461-b4b5-3c1803f7a5f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392460}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bc44afbe-5d76-5e09-9981-f46801ba7f6a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392456} :answer-field/id #uuid "fb19f32f-0991-5b71-b98c-bdea9bdda5e4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392455}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "54c6b58b-7b12-5b44-8e47-3a85c2ee0cea" :question/math-academy-id "q-41981" :question/problem "Calculate $ABC$, where\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 5\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, recall the associative law\n\n$$\nABC=(AB)C=A(BC).\n$$\n\nNotice that $A$ is a $1\\times 2$ matrix, $B$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix, and $C$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix. Therefore, \n\n- multiplying $A$ by $B$, we will obtain a $1\\times 2$ matrix, which will require finding $2$ entries, while\n\n- multiplying $B$ by $C$, we will obtain a $2\\times 2$ matrix, which will require finding $4$ entries.\n\nSo, we find the product $AB$ first: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 5\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 11\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply $AB$ by $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ABC & =(AB)C \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 11\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129781 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "98d6004a-547a-5b0b-9748-9571cc3ee632" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Tangent or Cotangent Using the Pythagorean Identity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c61dc7c0-d3f8-5a52-a767-d5a5950ed61b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\tan θ =-4\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128138} {:answer/id #uuid