$x=0$ without taking the pencil off of the paper."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129805 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d02297ca-0132-591b-9a69-bc2f93c95e2f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Jump Discontinuities on a Graph when Multiple Types of Discontinuity Exist"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9c8e4bae-40f9-57da-a3da-ff0669f18324" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x =-3$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128282} {:answer/id #uuid "3b22443a-3143-5730-93f6-8ddcba6d2ec5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x =-3$, $x = 0$ and $x = 1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128283} {:answer/id #uuid "4811bea4-80b9-55af-8dc6-9ae022c5adb1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x =-3$ and $x = 1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128284} {:answer/id #uuid "53d65e16-a75a-5231-91dd-7a5156be42ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x = 0$ and $x = 1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128285} {:answer/id #uuid "69b09b63-8d50-5bff-a999-f9151f885616" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x = 1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128286}] :answer-field/id #uuid "ce30e210-27f9-5333-a2f4-32f325604d71" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128281}] :question/id #uuid "2ed69968-e37f-592e-8251-96c027066333" :question/math-academy-id "q-48119" :question/problem "![](images/f1/f1e1a140e78362efddf48ba6744e2f88d109000fba8eae97818b024c2d249ad5.png)\n\nFor which points does the function $y = f(x)$ above have a jump discontinuity?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392628 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bbfddefc-d68b-5b1e-9ec5-fd18be204d92" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Semi-Open Intervals of Continuity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9a78c267-c941-5eaa-96d6-33cfa7f0d391" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/89/895c64a995d7f07a161a43945c369395b5cb365c5e7ec0f664df130c49795af8.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392630} {:answer/id #uuid "7ff21433-7f43-5f35-8e89-1765d909603a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/3c/3c24d4207dc777027919d211e4b2262d7340caf25a4166fa004e6e8129da5614.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392631} {:answer/id #uuid "c2952ac4-b61d-5673-a0b6-f777246f57dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/3c/3c5fedec0f96ef81c33146504cdabc8f0400ba79f77591167a285d119d238fd2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392632} {:answer/id #uuid "e15388cc-ed99-5129-8d21-2895051e2d94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/7f/7f2b4da10350a6ba230e0ec1b0b26822b133ee4a9c1228a61565ed559efcdd64.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392633} {:answer/id #uuid "7bb4212f-8084-572e-b47a-2ad475a715c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/38/387ed50e8983c83f96ea03bdd7a47d13d33a0c6e0728b52512fef8296351f73e.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392634}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7ff21433-7f43-5f35-8e89-1765d909603a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/3c/3c24d4207dc777027919d211e4b2262d7340caf25a4166fa004e6e8129da5614.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392631} :answer-field/id #uuid "454e920f-edd9-5ffd-abac-384d24521b07" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392629}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0790ffcc-e651-53c9-a00a-a9cbd12d21cb" :question/math-academy-id "q-48279" :question/problem "Which of the following functions is continuous on the interval $(1,3]?$" :question/worked-solution "The correct answer is the following.\n\n![](images/3c/3c24d4207dc777027919d211e4b2262d7340caf25a4166fa004e6e8129da5614.png)\n\nIndeed, we have that $f(x)$ is continuous on the interval $(1,3].$\n\nNotice that:\n\n- $f(x)$, $h(x)$,  and $q(x)$ are not right-continuous at $x=3.$\n\n- $p(x)$ is not continuous at  $x=2$, and so is not continuous on $(1,3].$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078996 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "36524faf-ac61-5756-89e3-c83a947ac61b" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Closed Intervals of Continuity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7f0baec8-e82b-5f18-bfe9-6bb24454a26d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/3d/3dfb0f9b6290f54d1261f0487c738056b7e1cde829ce3df0fb23af262e9e320b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392636} {:answer/id #uuid "c1852681-1420-5284-940b-d1399c8a616a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/72/72d7fd3effd14aa4449bae360aabd3577846653b5ec364933356e5ba40499cae.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392637} {:answer/id #uuid "a9e0a975-3edc-51c3-a886-10ff5f3cfdff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/b5/b58a88020b68d05c001a188faf7f3c4b1f1b57f8f9a9854d86ec2dc44b6bc347.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392638} {:answer/id #uuid "58e8cc04-c123-5e69-bb13-8156198731dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/e5/e57af2e43bd292195f617f60ffed8055891531a2ca5d0249706497d7932d6639.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392639} {:answer/id #uuid "05aeba16-6981-571b-8fd2-c8fb8c802213" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/97/978bb8f022b5a8ff96a47c79c5db11f455511addfaea55cb27f92ccc1c133072.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392640}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "58e8cc04-c123-5e69-bb13-8156198731dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/e5/e57af2e43bd292195f617f60ffed8055891531a2ca5d0249706497d7932d6639.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392639} :answer-field/id #uuid "7b9e37d1-a2f5-593f-a51a-6ff6525396de" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392635}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b9688522-541f-5cbf-8ad0-7cde48d13425" :question/math-academy-id "q-48293" :question/problem "Which of the following functions is continuous on the interval $[-1,2]?$" :question/worked-solution "The correct answer is the following. \n\n![](images/e5/e57af2e43bd292195f617f60ffed8055891531a2ca5d0249706497d7932d6639.png)\n\n      \n\nIndeed, this function is continuous on the open intervals $(-\\infty ,2)$ and $(2,\\infty ).$\n\nAdditionally, the function is left-continuous at $x=2.$ Hence, it is continuous on the interval $(-\\infty ,2].$\n\nSo, we can say that the function is continuous on the intervals $(-\\infty ,2]$ and  $(2,\\infty ).$ It is also continuous on any subinterval of these intervals.\n\nTherefore, this function is continuous on $[-1,2],$ because $[-1,2]$ is contained inside the interval $(-\\infty ,2].$\n\nNotice that:\n\n- $g(x)$ and $p(x)$ are not left-continuous at $x=2.$\n\n- $h(x)$ is not defined at $x=-1,$ and so it is not continuous on the interval $[-1,2].$\n\n- $q(x)$ is not defined at $x=1,$ and so it is not continuous on the interval $[-1,2].$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392641 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bbfddefc-d68b-5b1e-9ec5-fd18be204d92" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Semi-Open Intervals of Continuity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10ef9b73-f52e-5b9c-9db2-9010a5191176" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/72/7225a16c8dfa80bc750607b30c6deb30bb82807365c0c975c6b5ae3e33e11c6f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392643} {:answer/id #uuid "d19ed205-b0ad-5bbb-bcec-dfa49650fec0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/da/da6eb0e1cc28a0bef252e0d66bb232006f6a6e54142b27bb04401ca97025af30.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392644} {:answer/id #uuid "f034c628-7593-57d9-af24-2336a12e680e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/6c/6c04be0224db916731c3f7b4e2093887ffd36a258b19c4112766125ff435704c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392645} {:answer/id #uuid "899a69d0-95f3-5220-8187-28e906306a43" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/49/49356dbd9984d40899b321698d641f200d1d28d498323aa88fdfdfac53ed7910.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392646} {:answer/id #uuid "c336072e-08e4-5982-8f58-51b86e8995ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/13/1326b043af92c5945a5fa679f7daa57eb269ae13edb6d3f9296b3a522678039c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392647}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "10ef9b73-f52e-5b9c-9db2-9010a5191176" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/72/7225a16c8dfa80bc750607b30c6deb30bb82807365c0c975c6b5ae3e33e11c6f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392643} :answer-field/id #uuid "d4d2a845-2450-5e73-b0b2-bcfee6b03964" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392642}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c74056bd-b0fc-5dc2-914f-2bbc6b987726" :question/math-academy-id "q-48348" :question/problem "Which of the following functions is continuous on the interval $(-\\infty ,-1]?$  You may assume that all points of discontinuity for each function are shown." :question/worked-solution "The correct answer is the following.\n\n![](images/72/7225a16c8dfa80bc750607b30c6deb30bb82807365c0c975c6b5ae3e33e11c6f.png)\n\nWe see that $h(x)$ is continuous on the interval $(-\\infty ,0],$ and since $(-\\infty ,-1]$ lies completely inside $(-\\infty ,0],$ it is also continuous on $(-\\infty ,-1].$\n\nNotice that:\n\n- $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ are not continuous at $x=-2$, and so are not continuous on $(-\\infty ,-1].$\n\n- $p(x)$ and $q(x)$ are not continuous at $x=-3$, and so are not continuous on $(-\\infty ,-1].$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392648 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1659900c-f6c4-5880-9d2a-4ee68c390432" :knowledge-point/title "Relating the Rate of Change of a Diagonal Length to Another Rate of Change"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "797a4f0c-845d-5bd4-a00e-f4042c09b16b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12.5\\text{ feet}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392650} {:answer/id #uuid "c6bd86db-1af1-529e-b7e3-3d8c2f9d1330" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\text{ feet}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392651} {:answer/id #uuid "4ff2b416-758c-5d4f-944b-1eac169b1cce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\text{ feet}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392652} {:answer/id #uuid "bb340c74-6d19-51d7-b6b4-c00f66ac9476" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\text{ feet}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392653} {:answer/id #uuid "71d4db78-dc17-5a6a-ab36-7c6870d8b79b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.5\\text{ feet}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392654}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bb340c74-6d19-51d7-b6b4-c00f66ac9476" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\text{ feet}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392653} :answer-field/id #uuid "50391d24-edeb-556b-9cad-499093215c2a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392649}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7963534b-05e1-5074-b217-dce61952496a" :question/math-academy-id "q-48381" :question/problem "The sides (in feet) of a square are increasing. At a particular moment, the rate of increase of the area of the square is numerically equal to four times the rate of increase of the diagonals. What is the length of the diagonals of the square at that moment?" :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ be the area of the square, and $h$ be the length of the diagonal. The relationship between $A$ and $h$ is\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}h}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe given information tells us that $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=4\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}$, so replacing $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}$ in the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =h\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 4\\cancel{\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}} & =h\\cancel{\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}} \\\\ 4 & =h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo at that particular moment, we have that the length of the diagonals of the square is $4\\text{ feet}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392655 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "999d8587-8f15-5871-81dd-26c0c0ae063e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Functions Presented Graphically That Satisfy the Mean Value Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4416f6af-e7e0-5f20-baa8-a14cec8e89ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/36/36a329b531e3d48d82585ee336b10dff8b4b78585829a56d72cdac61e0aba45d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392657} {:answer/id #uuid "fbe74343-d1c1-5cfa-81f4-3673e6df0173" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/66/66663e046f3c00d3f4884b7129cdc5b5d4be18574c6f2fc47fd39dcc976e398c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392658} {:answer/id #uuid "7c3a2940-5b1a-5abf-bb7b-3ffa5c3936e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/78/78a7985112e69fa2a647ad4548791ef42bee13c458d1e160199f767771315513.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392659} {:answer/id #uuid "cfd74d22-e811-51ce-be7f-5b74e127d042" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/fa/fab6a73c970500b896e38e75faf21c1fd5aecf9909d9537963e269fd5f60c70d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392660} {:answer/id #uuid "f2c2d1ac-2bc4-52aa-a56c-b9556db71fcb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/e6/e6ccc7f465d50c13059b93e1b12b80bc3c10b9970ea0fedd5a3b3b9d4d3cabf5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392661}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f2c2d1ac-2bc4-52aa-a56c-b9556db71fcb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/e6/e6ccc7f465d50c13059b93e1b12b80bc3c10b9970ea0fedd5a3b3b9d4d3cabf5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392661} :answer-field/id #uuid "37587bec-ab50-5693-886d-2f7966abfa74" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392656}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2b51b4f8-9d77-5aa5-bb70-80904cd708eb" :question/math-academy-id "q-48399" :question/problem "Which of the following functions satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[0,2]?$" :question/worked-solution "For a function to satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[0,2],$ the function must be continuous on $[0,2]$ and differentiable on $(0,2).$\n\nFirst, notice that the function $f(x)$ is continuous on $[0,2]$ and differentiable on $(0,2)$, so it satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\nLet's check each of the other functions individually.\n\n- The function $t(x)$  has a vertical asymptote at $x=1$ and is not defined for $x\\in (1,\\infty ).$ Hence, it is not continuous on $[0,2].$ Therefore, $t(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $g(x)$  is continuous on $[0,2]$ but has a cusp at $x=1.$  Hence, it is not differentiable at $x=1,$ which means it is not differentiable on $(0,2).$  Therefore, $g(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function  $h(x)$ has a removable discontinuity at $x=1.$ Hence, it  is not continuous on $[0,2].$ Therefore, $h(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function  $s(x)$ is continuous on $[0,2]$ but has a corner point at $x=1.$   Hence, it is not differentiable at $x=1,$ which means it is not differentiable on $(0,2).$  Therefore, $s(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092739 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2f4bfd24-dafd-57b3-940a-154a6e0acf3d" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a General Exponential Function Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8d9ee625-e773-554a-b6fd-1b00c950d2f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2 \\cdot 3^{2x}\\ln (3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090605} {:answer/id #uuid "a2e4f469-6799-5ce4-b565-a59f324b5ec8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4x3^{2x^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090606} {:answer/id #uuid "bdacec05-5c20-55b6-8105-e82bd5158002" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x3^{2x^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090607} {:answer/id #uuid "f7139b42-c4a4-5c78-ab9a-4c8bcf3ecb8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4x3^{2x^{2}}\\ln (3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090608} {:answer/id #uuid "15755bfa-20fa-599b-b783-c67a495db7ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x3^{2x}\\ln (3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090609}] :answer-field/id #uuid "4acd11bc-cb2e-5a8e-a9ee-06ec6470c2d5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090604}] :question/id #uuid "8711f34a-9cc5-5a70-9af6-219e0592f067" :question/math-academy-id "q-48565" :question/problem "If $f(x) = 3^{2x^{2}}$, find $f^{′}(x)$."}]}
